\f1\fs22 Through streets so narrow and alleys so jagged they seemed woven from tangles of broken buildings bandaged with tired tarps and bound shut with cable and wire, an Elvi commando squad escorted Chanta and Sarah deeper into the Pale. Scouts ranged ahead, sweepers trailed behind, and four or five more flanked the perimeter, combing their way forward with the assiduousness of men combing their own hair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah thought the Elvi were overreacting, or at least over-acting, which, after the gangster-style violence in the Memphis Mafia Bar, did nothing to settle her nerves.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta, watching Sarah\'92s face cycle from radiant to stark with each new layer of squalor, papered over his own wild emotions with the best impervious detachment he could fake. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With nothing else to offer, he became tour guide. \'93The heart of the Pale swallowed all the roads years ago; only narrow paths and tunnels are left.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That sounds delightfully urban,\'94 Sarah replied. \'93Where are we going, anyway?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Vegas Show tonight,\'94 one of the Elvi whispered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta glowed brighter than a Christmas tree. \'93We\'92re going to a Vegas Show?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i The
\f1\i0  Vegas Show,\'94 the Elvis corrected. \'93We celebrate once a year for our momma\'92s birthday.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s today?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No. It\'92s next month.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked. \'93PAPER agents want us dead, and we\'92re going to a show?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Safest place in the Ghetto tonight.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think those creepy wizard dudes are following us,\'94 Chanta muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They are,\'94 the Elvis agreed, deadpan.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re not worried about them?\'94 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis grunted and grimaced. \'93They\'92re more interested in you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What did they want anyway?\'94 Chanta asked, sticking on another layer of I\'92m Cool.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They want to get me pregnant.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi patrol halted, side-eyes flickering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They wanna make you a momma?\'94 the Elvis echoed, grave as a funeral host.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged. \'93I\'92ve had worse propositions.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stomped about, trying to douse his own white-hot confusion. \'93You can\'92t be\'97no\'97what the tax? I\'97 you\'97 they\'97what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Steady, Sir Galahad. I realised years ago I could never be pregnant.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi shifted their feet, looking ready for either a fight or a hymn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta felt the mood darken. \'93Where are we going again?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Graceland,\'94 the Elvis said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was beside himself with glee. \'93We\'92re going to Graceland!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chilo, stop rushing ahead,\'94 Kleo chided, years of exasperation condensed into a whisper. \'93They\'92ll catch us following.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo needn\'92t have worried. The throngs of Elvi swelled, and grew steely-eyed and thick. The air itself was humming with electricity for something big: a festival mood, knots of celebrants outside bars and clubs, everyone drinking, everyone waiting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They lost sight of Sarah and Chanta for a moment as the tide of bodies swept the Sages back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There they go!\'94 Chilo pointed. \'93Down that side alley.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The alley emptied into a market square, every stall, tent, and flatbed showing off the latest in Elvi style, which is to say, the same jump suits, leather, cowboy, and military getups they\'92d worn since before emancipation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Sages hung back as Sarah and Chanta were ushered into one of the less-ramshackle fashion stores.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yer going to the Vegas Show?\'94 asked an aging Elvis, picking his teeth beside a truck.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Excuse me?\'94 Solo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Vegas Show\'97the biggest night of the year. Seems your friends are fixing to go if they\'92re buying proper evening wear.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Evening wear,\'94 Solo repeated, as if the phrase alone could reconcile what he was seeing: cosplay sold out of the back of a truck. His toes felt like ice cubes in his slippers. As his knees knocked and he shivered in his onesie against the near-midnight autumn air, he cursed the random arrival times of the Travelator, and regretted ever believing in prophecy or, for that matter, trusting a weather app.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You can\'92t show up to Graceland looking like asylum escapees. Sure, you could join all them tourists in the stores, but why would you? You can get the exact same high-quality merchandise at half the price from my very fine emporium right here.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You mean your truck,\'94 Kleo clarified.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes indeed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo shrugged. From a rack off the tailgate, he picked a jumpsuit of many colours.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That one comes with a free cape,\'94 the proprietor said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, I think\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yer taking the cape,\'94 the proprietor insisted, aggression rising. \'93To do otherwise\'92d be sacrilegious.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo procured a matching leather jacket and pants; Chilo opted for the army ensemble.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo blinked, surrendered. \'93Where\'92s the dressing room?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yer standing in it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Kleo looked around. Everywhere, Elvi of all genders, were stripping and trying on outfits. The reflections off all the sequins and rhinestones were like a swarm of fireflies. He took a deep breath, stripped and dressed. The suit fit well enough, so he tossed his pyjamas into a nearby oil-drum fire, relieved he would not need to undress again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let\'92s see, boys,\'94 the proprietor said as the Sages tightened buttons and adjusted capes to underwhelmed satisfaction. \'93Line up!\'94 From a box in the truck\'92s footwell, he produced three large syringes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What are those?\'94 Solo asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Epipens. Epinephrine auto-injectors. For anaphylactic shock. None of you got allergies?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What, like peanuts?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax, forbid. The air alone would kill you.\'94 The proprietor slipped an injector into each inner jacket pocket. They fit perfectly. \'93You won\'92t get in without \'92em. Big night, big crowds\'85 if it happens\'85 well, you\'92ll know.\'94
